		(uuid "782b5b4a-8088-4c32-90b8-18d9e583f21d")
	)
	(text_box "The LDO input filtering should reduce the high-frequency switching noise enough that the LDO PSRR can be effective.\n\n@{\"Id\":\"YSAAIAPV\\YVRIKKLD\",\"ObjectType\":2} & @{\"Id\":\"YSAAIAPV\\HAPMQBTT\",\"ObjectType\":2} are needed to dampen resonant LC peaks (lower the Q).\n\n@{\"Id\":\"YSAAIAPV\\HAPMQBTT\",\"ObjectType\":2} will sag the DC voltage. Can increase @{\"Id\":\"YSAAIAPV\\HAPMQBTT\",\"ObjectType\":2} to dampen nosie further but this will create a larger voltage drop which can become noise at higher dI/dT events. Needs to be tested to see whether the tradeoff is worth it.\n\nEstimated analog current draw is ~\n5mA.\nAt 100mA, the LDO input voltage should only sag to ~~7V at DC with @{\"Id\":\"YSAAIAPV\\HAPMQBTT\",\"ObjectType\":2} = 20Ω. Minimum LDO input voltage = 5.3V\n\nMajor sources of current draw are the OP-AMP quiescent current and ADC analog rail. The OP-AMPs are driving high-impedance loads so current transients due to the signal amplification should be minimized. ADC current draw should therefore be relatively constant. Even then, transients in the audio range are well within the LDO effective PSRR range."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 111.76 227.33 0)
		(size -104.14 43.18)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
			(color 0 0 0 1)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 255 150 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
